{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 26,
  "title": "Drinks",
  "description": "Name the drinks, and ask for one at the bar.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Drinks are nouns like any other, and the bar is where a learner first uses them. Two of them are phrases, and one of them keeps el even though it is a feminine word.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The nouns",
        "body": "El zumo, el vino, el refresco, el ponche and el café con leche are masculine. La cerveza, la limonada, la infusión and la horchata are feminine. El agua con gas is the exception that proves the rule: agua is feminine, but a stressed final -a takes el, exactly like el agua and el agua mineral.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "el zumo",
            "en": "the juice"
          },
          {
            "es": "la cerveza",
            "en": "the beer"
          },
          {
            "es": "el agua con gas",
            "en": "the sparkling water"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "At the bar",
        "body": "You ask for a drink with un or una and the name of the drink: un zumo, una cerveza, un café con leche. A cold drink is un zumo frío, and the adjective comes after the noun and changes to match it.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "quiero una cerveza fría",
            "en": "I want a cold beer"
          },
          {
            "es": "un café con leche, por favor",
            "en": "a coffee with milk, please"
          },
          {
            "es": "el zumo es natural",
            "en": "the juice is natural"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
